  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nHigh pressure was centered just to the northeast of Lake\nSuperior early this afternoon and extended across all of the\nUpper Midwest. Weak upper level troughing was in place as well,\nleading to some light shower activity across southern Minnesota\nper radar and surface observations. This activity is weakening\nas it moves north and is not expected to affect the Northland.\nClearing skies are expected heading into tonight with winds\nbecoming light and variable. Temperatures are expected to fall\ninto the middle to lower 30s away from Lake Superior leading to\nwidespread frost across the region. Some areas along and north\nof the Iron Range and across the Penokee Range may reach\nfreezing or just below, but a hard freeze is not expected. A\nFrost Advisory will be needed.\n\nAs the area of high pressure moves to the east tomorrow,\nsoutherly return flow will setup across the Upper Midwest and\nsignal the start of a slow warming trend into the weekend. Highs\nfor the remainder of the week will be in the 60s with some upper\n50s Tuesday through Wednesday along the North Shore with onshore\nflow expected. Lows tomorrow night will remain on the cool side\nin the middle to upper 30s and another Frost Advisory may be\nneeded for areas inland of Lake Superior.\n\nUpper ridging will setup across the central CONUS Wednesday into\nThursday with a shortwave pushing eastward underneath for\nWednesday night into Thursday. This may lead to some shower\nactivity during this time, but dry air in the low levels and\nhigh pressure to the east will work against them. Additional\nrain chances will be possible over the weekend as another\nshortwave moves through, but overall chances look low. Some 70s\nwill be possible by Sunday and Monday, mainly across northwest\nWisconsin.",
